          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the lac operon, what is the role of the CAP–cAMP complex when glucose levels are low?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It represses transcription by binding to the operator sequence</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The repressor, not CAP, binds the operator. The CAP–cAMP complex acts at the promoter to provide positive control of transcription.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It enhances RNA polymerase binding at the promoter, boosting transcription</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. When glucose is low, cAMP rises and binds CAP. This complex helps RNA polymerase initiate transcription, ensuring bacteria use glucose first before lactose.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It converts lactose into the inducer allolactose</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allolactose is produced from lactose by beta-galactosidase, not by the CAP–cAMP complex. CAP–cAMP acts at the promoter to boost transcription.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It prevents the repressor from binding allolactose</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Allolactose binds the repressor directly, without involvement of CAP. The CAP–cAMP complex provides separate positive control at the promoter level.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is that CAP–cAMP enhances RNA polymerase binding at the promoter. This positive control ensures strong transcription of the lac operon only when glucose is low and lactose is present."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The CAP–cAMP complex provides positive control by helping RNA polymerase start transcription when glucose is low — ensuring bacteria preferentially use glucose."
                }
              ],
