            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the polymerase chain reaction, why is Taq polymerase used instead of ordinary DNA polymerase?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It synthesises DNA more accurately than other polymerases</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Taq polymerase actually lacks proofreading ability, so it is less accurate. Its key advantage is heat stability — it survives repeated denaturation at 94°C.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It is heat-stable and survives repeated denaturation at 94°C</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Taq polymerase from the thermophilic bacterium Thermus aquaticus remains active after repeated heating, so it only needs to be added once at the start of the reaction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It does not require primers to initiate DNA synthesis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>All DNA polymerases, including Taq, require primers. Taq's advantage is its ability to withstand the high temperatures used to denature DNA in each PCR cycle.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It can directly use RNA as a template</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Taq polymerase copies DNA, not RNA. Reverse transcriptase must first convert RNA to cDNA. Taq's critical advantage is that it survives the 94°C denaturation step.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
