            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which enzyme, present in most mammals but absent in humans, converts uric acid into the more soluble allantoin?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Xanthine oxidase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Xanthine oxidase is present in humans — it produces uric acid from xanthine. The enzyme humans lack is uricase, which would convert uric acid further to allantoin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Adenosine deaminase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Adenosine deaminase converts adenosine to inosine in purine degradation. Its deficiency causes SCID, not gout. Humans lack uricase, which converts uric acid to allantoin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Uricase</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Humans and other great apes lack uricase (urate oxidase), making uric acid the final product of purine metabolism. Above about 7 mg/dL, urate crystals can cause gout.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Purine nucleoside phosphorylase</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PNP removes ribose from nucleosides, converting inosine to hypoxanthine. Its deficiency selectively destroys T cells. The enzyme absent in humans is uricase.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The answer is uricase. Humans lack this enzyme, so uric acid accumulates as the final product. When it exceeds about 7 mg/dL, urate crystals can deposit in joints, causing gout."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Humans lack uricase, so uric acid is our end product of purine degradation — explaining why gout is largely a human disease."
                }
              ],
